  "worked_solution": "The function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where both $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. The vector function $g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where its components are differentiable.  \n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. \n\nFor the real-valued function $u(t),$ we observe that it is differentiable over all values of $t.$\n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component, $\\sqrt{t+4}$,  is differentiable over $(-4,\\infty )$ since its derivative is not defined at $t=-4$ and the function is not defined for $t<-4.$\n\n- The second component,   ${sin}^{2}\u2061t$,  is differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\n- The third component, $\\sqrt[5]{t}$, is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the domains of all three components of  $g(t)$ is $(-4,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$  \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-4,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$",
